{
    "background_story": "Cookie was a passionate civil rights activist in the 1960s American South. Born into a family of sharecroppers, she witnessed firsthand the systemic disfranchisement of African Americans in rural communities. Despite the dangers, Cookie traveled from town to town, registering voters and advocating for equal rights. Her efforts were met with resistance from local authorities, who sought to silence her through intimidation and violence. Undeterred, Cookie continued to organize, using her charisma and conviction to inspire others to join the fight. Her work helped to expose the injustices of disfranchisement, paving the way for landmark legislation like the Voting Rights Act. Though she faced many challenges, Cookie remained committed to empowering marginalized communities, leaving a lasting legacy in the struggle for equality.",
